{"id":"min-cost-climbing-stairs","title":"Min Cost Climbing Stairs","difficulty":"Easy","pattern":"1-D Dynamic Programming","tags":["Dynamic Programming","Array"],"companies":{"google":2,"apple":1,"amazon":3,"microsoft":1},"description":"`cost[i]` is the price of stepping on stair `i`. Once you pay for a stair you may climb 1 or 2 stairs onward. You may begin on stair `0` or stair `1`, and the goal is to get past the last stair (to position `len(cost)`).\n\nReturn the minimum total cost.","examples":[{"input":"cost = [10,15,20]","output":"15","explanation":"Start on stair 1 (pay 15) and jump two steps to the top."},{"input":"cost = [1,100,1,1,1,100,1,1,100,1]","output":"6","explanation":"Start on stair 0 and take every stair that costs 1, skipping each 100: 1+1+1+1+1+1 = 6."}],"constraints":["2 <= cost.length <= 1000","0 <= cost[i] <= 999"],"hints":["Let f(i) be the cheapest way to reach position i (standing just past stair i-1, not yet paid for stair i).","f(i) = min(f(i-1) + cost[i-1], f(i-2) + cost[i-2]); f(0) = f(1) = 0 because you may start on either of the first two stairs for free.","Only the last two values are needed, so roll two variables."],"function":"minCostClimbingStairs","signature":"def minCostClimbingStairs(self, cost: List[int]) -> int:","arg_types":{"cost":"List[int]"},"return_type":"int","compare":"exact","tests":[{"input":{"cost":[10,15,20]},"output":15},{"input":{"cost":[1,100,1,1,1,100,1,1,100,1]},"output":6},{"input":{"cost":[5,3]},"output":3},{"input":{"cost":[0,0,0,1]},"output":0},{"input":{"cost":[1,2,3,4,5]},"output":6},{"input":{"cost":[0,37,74,10,47,84,20,57,94,30,67,3,40,77,13,50,87,23,60,97,33,70,6,43,80,16,53,90,26,63,100,36,73,9,46,83,19,56,93,29,66,2,39,76,12,49,86,22,59,96,32,69,5,42,79,15,52,89,25,62,99,35,72,8,45,82,18,55,92,28,65,1,38,75,11,48,85,21,58,95,31,68,4,41,78,14,51,88,24,61,98,34,71,7,44,81,17,54,91,27,64,0,37,74,10,47,84,20,57,94,30,67,3,40,77,13,50,87,23,60,97,33,70,6,43,80,16,53,90,26,63,100,36,73,9,46,83,19,56,93,