37,74,10,47,84,20,57,94,30,67,3,40,77,13,50,87,23,60,97,33,70,6,43,80,16,53,90,26,63,100,36,73,9,46,83,19,56,93,29,66,2,39,76,12,49,86,22,59,96,32]},"output":99824}],"solution":"class Solution:\n    def minCostClimbingStairs(self, cost: List[int]) -> int:\n        two_back, one_back = 0, 0  # cheapest cost to reach positions i-2 and i-1\n        for c in cost:\n            two_back, one_back = one_back, c + min(one_back, two_back)\n        return min(one_back, two_back)\n","approach":"**Define `f(i)` as the minimum cost to be standing on stair `i` (having paid for it).** You arrived from `i-1` or `i-2`, so `f(i) = cost[i] + min(f(i-1), f(i-2))`, with `f(0) = cost[0]` and `f(1) = cost[1]`. The top lies past the last stair, reachable from either of the last two, so the answer is `min(f(n-1), f(n-2))`.\n\nThe loop above folds the base cases in by starting both rolling values at 0 (standing before the staircase costs nothing) and treating `cost[i]` as paid on arrival.\n\n**Why it works:** every path to the top ends on stair n-1 or n-2, and the cheapest way to each stair depends only on the cheapest way to the two stairs below it (optimal substructure), so the greedy local min is globally optimal.\n\n**Common mistakes:** forgetting that the goal is *past* the last stair (returning f(n-1) alone), or forcing a start at stair 0.\n\n**Interview follow-ups:** Reconstruct the stairs stepped on? (Keep a `choice[i]` flag recording which predecessor won, then walk back from the cheaper of the last two.) Allow steps up to k? (Sliding-window minimum with a monotonic deque gives O(n).) What if some stairs are closed? (Set their cost to infinity.)","complexity":{"time":"O(n)","space":"O(1)"},"similar":["climbing-stairs","house-robber"],"number":121,"starter":"class Solution:\n    def minCostClimbingStairs(self, cost: List[int]) -> int:\n        pass\n"}